e><issue>3</issue><fpage>306</fpage><lpage>14</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7932792</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="f1-ebo-2-277" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>A graphical depiction of the relative shapes of Equations (13), (14), and (16).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="EBO-2-277-g001"/></fig><fig id="f2-ebo-2-277" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The number of quadrature points versus the relative error in the sample molecular evolution integration problem.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="EBO-2-277-g002"/></fig><fig id="f3-ebo-2-277" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Gaussian quadrature weights and abscissae <italic>n</italic> = 20 for points for the sample molecular evolution integration problem.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="EBO-2-277-g003"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ebo-2-277" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Exact recursion coefficients for selected probability distributions. For Equations (6) and (7), we scale the recursion coefficients such that <italic>a</italic><italic><sub>i</sub></italic>= <italic>&#x003bb;</italic> &#x000b7; <italic>a</italic>&#x02032;<italic><sub>i</sub></italic> and <italic>b</italic><italic><sub>i</sub></italic> = <italic>&#x003bb;</italic><sup>2</sup> &#x000b7; <italic>b</italic>&#x02032;<italic><sub>i</sub></italic>. Note that for <italic>n</italic> recursion coefficients, at least the first 2<italic>n</italic> moments must exist. There is also a special case for the Beta distribution:<italic>a</italic><italic><sub>0</sub></italic> = 1/2 when <italic>&#x003b1;</italic> = <italic>&#x003b2;</italic> = 1 (the uniform distribution).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="EBO-2-277-g00t1"/></table-wrap></floats-group></article>